From: Jahshaka <jahshaka@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Hlms: the shader hash's field sizes, measured and rebalanced (3/16/13)

WHY (Jahshaka lane HLMSBITS-1, 2026-09-15 — the structural half of the
2026-09-14 crash that lane SHADERCACHE-2 fixed the cause of)

Every shader lookup in Ogre-Next is a 32-bit hash of three packed fields:

    [ Hlms type : 3 ][ renderable index : 21 ][ pass index : 8 ]        upstream

`Hlms::getMaterial` ORs a renderable half and a pass half together, and nothing
checks that the halves fit their fields. The pass index has EIGHT of them — 256
distinct sets of pass properties for the life of a process, guarded by an
`assert()` that every release build compiles out. Entry 256 does not saturate
and does not wrap in place: it carries into the RENDERABLE field beside it, and
the next reader of that field indexes `mRenderableCache` out of range. That is
exactly how the owner's editor and two rig instances died on 2026-09-14
(HlmsDiskCache::copyFrom -> Hlms::getProperty -> std::lower_bound, SIGSEGV at
address 0, from the periodic shader-cache save; the first guard line decodes as
renderable 3228 of 3227 = pass 86 + 512). SHADERCACHE-2 removed the DRIVER that
got there (a sky capture cube named uniquely per capture, hashed into the pass
properties as `target_envprobe_map`); the 8-bit field it overflowed stayed.

MEASURED, BECAUSE 256 IS EITHER GENEROUS OR ABSURD AND GUESSING IS NOT ALLOWED.
The pin was instrumented (a log line on every growth of either cache, with the
property diff that caused it) and driven through one session that opened the
eight shipped samples, walked Low -> Epic on each, drove every World Mode row
through every one of its options, changed the sky forty times, entered and left
play, took screenshots at three grades and toggled planar reflections:

    Hlms                  pass cache peak      renderable cache peak
    HlmsPbs                    115                     24
    HlmsLowLevel                94                    104
    HlmsUnlit                   12                     16

115 of 256 — 45 % of the field, from ONE session, on an app whose pass
properties legitimately include the render-pass shape (view, shadow atlas,
reflection, probe capture, prepass, SSR, refractions), the MSAA count, the
shadow filter and its PCF iterations, the Forward+ cluster geometry, the GI
technique and every reflection/probe TARGET NAME. The properties that differed
most often on a new PBS pass entry, in order: has_planar_reflections,
hlms_gen_normals_gbuffer, hlms_prepass, target_envprobe_map,
StrongMacroblockBits, pcf/pcf_iterations, fwd_clustered_lights_per_cell,
hlms_msaa_samples, hlms_use_ssr, irradiance_field, vct_disable_diffuse.
A warm boot re-populates the cache from the disk cache BEFORE a frame is drawn
(measured: 79 of the previous session's entries replayed by
HlmsDiskCache::applyTo), so the working set a session starts from is not zero.

THE SPLIT, and why this one:

    [ Hlms type : 3 ][ renderable index : 16 ][ pass index : 13 ]        ours

  * PASS 13 bits = 8,192 entries = 71x the measured peak, and 32x a session
    eight times as varied as the one measured. This is the side with the
    pressure, the side with no eviction, and the side an app can re-enter.
  * RENDERABLE 16 bits = 65,536 entries = 630x the measured peak. This cache
    counts distinct MATERIAL+MESH property sets, and every entry costs a shader
    compile per pass entry it meets — 65,536 of them is far past anything a
    machine will ever compile, which is why the bits are better spent on the
    pass side.
  * TYPE stays 3: HLMS_MAX is 8 and a static_assert now says so.
  * The three fields tile the uint32 exactly, asserted at compile time.

AND THE FIELDS NOW REFUSE INSTEAD OF OVERFLOWING. `assert` is not a guard in a
release build. There is ONE appender for the pass cache now
(`Hlms::findOrAddPassCache`) where there were three open-coded copies —
Hlms::preparePassHashFinal, HlmsUnlit::preparePassHash (which had no warning of
any kind, only the compiled-out assert) and HlmsDiskCache::applyTo — and it:
  * THROWS on the render path, naming the Hlms, the count and the field width,
    because there is no correct shader to return once the index cannot be
    addressed and a wrong one is worse than an exception;
  * RETURNS FALSE when a disk cache is being replayed, so a too-large cache file
    costs a slower start instead of a dead boot;
  * WARNS ONCE at three quarters full, while there is still room to act on it,
    naming the unique-name class of bug that causes unbounded growth.
`Hlms::addRenderableCache` refuses the same way. `Hlms::getMaterial` and
getMaterialSerial01 additionally assert `(renderableHalf & passHalf) == 0` — one
AND that says "these fields overlap" whatever the split happens to be.

THE RENDER QUEUE'S SORT KEY IS PART OF THIS PATCH, and it has to be: RqBits
gives the shader 10 bits and `addRenderable` filled them by MASKING THE LOW BITS
of the renderable hash. Those low bits are the hole the pass field lives in —
two bits of the renderable index at upstream's split, and NOTHING AT ALL (a
constant, so the whole shader term of the sort key vanishes) at any split whose
pass field is at least ShaderBits wide. The comment beside RqBits::ShaderBits
has always claimed "the higher 3 bits contain HlmsTypes"; now they do. The field
is composed from the hash's TYPE and RENDERABLE fields explicitly, so objects
drawn by the same shader sort together again and this line never depends on the
split again. Draw order changes (it was ordering by two arbitrary bits before);
pixels do not — opaque draws are depth-tested and transparent ones sort by depth
ABOVE the shader term. Proven, see below.

CRUD: `HlmsBits::InputLayoutShift` / `InputLayoutMask` are deleted. They were
declared in the header and defined nowhere — any use was a link error — and the
only references in the tree are two commented-out lines in HlmsDiskCache. The
constants also become `constexpr` in the header (the compile-time asserts need
them there, and every TU that shifts by them now gets an immediate).

TWO PUBLIC ACCESSORS ARE ADDED (`getPassCacheSize`, `getRenderableCacheSize`,
plus the two capacities): both vectors are protected, never evicted, and their
size is the health reading nobody could take until a crash took it for them.
Jahshaka reports them through `app.shaderCache()` and the shader-cache suite
asserts the number instead of the absence of a warning.

DISK FORMAT: unchanged, and it does NOT encode the layout — verified, not
assumed. HlmsDiskCache serialises pass and renderable PROPERTY VECTORS and
re-derives both indices on load (OgreHlmsDiskCache.cpp save/applyTo), and the
Vulkan microcode map is keyed by the shader SOURCE TEXT
(VulkanProgram::getNameForMicrocodeCache), not by the hash. `c_hlmsDiskCacheVersion`
therefore stays at 6. Jahshaka's own cache fingerprint folds a hash of this
patch directory, so every existing cache is discarded once anyway.

PIXELS: byte-identical. `--engine-selftest` =
b55e2d5d03dce4059b71a65f8e61dc4773fb0b048e3de05428aca83cabae0ec7 (unchanged);
13 of 13 Ogre-Next sample ports byte-identical from the same recorded pose
(PbsMaterials, LocalCubemaps, Refractions, Hdr, AreaApproxLights, IesProfiles,
ShadowMapFromCode, PlanarReflections, ScreenSpaceReflections, Tutorial_SSAO,
Tutorial_SMAA, Decals, ImageVoxelizer); 7 of the 8 shipped samples
byte-identical at two grades, the eighth (Particles) differing by the same
22,787 pixels between two runs of the UNPATCHED binary — its documented
open-to-open non-determinism, not this patch.

SOURCE: OgreMain + the Unlit component. Every tree must re-run build-ogre.sh.
It TOUCHES THE SAME FILES AS PATCH 0035 (OgreHlms.cpp, OgreHlmsDiskCache.cpp),
so a tree that already carries 0035 must reset its ogre-next submodule before
build-ogre.sh — the script's per-patch reverse check cannot see the overlap.

